{"id": "SATM28TRSSF", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The equation $kx + 5y = 60$ represents a line that passes through the point $(3, 6)$. What is the value of $k$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-10$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$6$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$10$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$30$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is $10$. Substitute the point $(3, 6)$ into $kx + 5y = 60$: $k(3) + 5(6) = 60$, which gives $3k + 30 = 60$. Subtracting $30$ from both sides yields $3k = 30$, and dividing by $3$ gives $k = 10$. The value $30$ results from stopping at the intermediate step $3k = 30$ without dividing by $3$. The value $-10$ comes from a sign error when dividing $30$ by $3$, yielding $-10$ instead of $10$. The value $6$ results from dividing $30$ by $5$ (the coefficient of $y$) instead of by $3$ (the coefficient of $k$ after substitution), giving $\\frac{30}{5} = 6$."}
{"id": "SATM28V6MKT", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Which of the following equations represents the line passing through the points $(1, 7)$ and $(4, -2)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = -3x + 4$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = -3x + 7$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -3x + 10$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = 3x + 4$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-2 - 7}{4 - 1} = \\frac{-9}{3} = -3$. Using point-slope form with $(1, 7)$: $y - 7 = -3(x - 1)$, which gives $y = -3x + 3 + 7 = -3x + 10$. The equation $y = -3x + 4$ results from incorrectly writing $y - 7 = -3(x + 1)$ instead of $-3(x - 1)$, yielding $y = -3x - 3 + 7 = -3x + 4$. The equation $y = -3x + 7$ results from assuming the $y$-coordinate of the first point is the $y$-intercept. The equation $y = 3x + 4$ results from computing the slope as $\\frac{7 - (-2)}{4 - 1} = \\frac{9}{3} = 3$ (omitting the sign), then using $y - 7 = 3(x - 1)$ to get $y = 3x + 4$."}
{"id": "SATM28WFWXL", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(3, 7)$ and $(6, 19)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-5", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{19 - 7}{6 - 3} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$. Using point-slope form with $(3, 7)$: $y - 7 = 4(x - 3)$, which gives $y = 4x - 5$. The $y$-intercept is $-5$."}
{"id": "SATM28WIBXX", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The line $5x - 8y = 40$ is graphed in the $xy$-plane. A second line is perpendicular to this line and passes through the point $(3, -2)$. What is the $y$-coordinate of the point where the second line crosses the $y$-axis?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{-34}{5}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{14}{5}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{34}{5}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{-14}{5}$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Rewriting $5x - 8y = 40$ in slope-intercept form: $y = \\frac{5}{8}x - 5$, so the slope is $\\frac{5}{8}$. The perpendicular slope is $-\\frac{8}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(3, -2)$: $y + 2 = -\\frac{8}{5}(x - 3)$, so $y = -\\frac{8}{5}x + \\frac{24}{5} - 2 = -\\frac{8}{5}x + \\frac{24}{5} - \\frac{10}{5} = -\\frac{8}{5}x + \\frac{14}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{14}{5}$. The distractor $\\frac{-34}{5}$ arises from using the original slope instead of the negative reciprocal and making a sign error in the calculation. The distractor $\\frac{34}{5}$ comes from using the positive reciprocal $\\frac{8}{5}$ instead of $-\\frac{8}{5}$. The distractor $\\frac{-14}{5}$ results from negating the correct answer due to a sign slip when distributing the perpendicular slope."}
{"id": "SATM28XTRBU", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ is defined by the equation $3x - 4y = 20$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(6, -3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-11$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$8$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $3x - 4y = 20$ becomes $y = \\frac{3}{4}x - 5$, so the slope of line $p$ is $\\frac{3}{4}$. A line perpendicular to $p$ has slope $-\\frac{4}{3}$. Using point-slope form with $(6, -3)$: $y - (-3) = -\\frac{4}{3}(x - 6)$, which gives $y + 3 = -\\frac{4}{3}x + 8$, so $y = -\\frac{4}{3}x + 5$. The $y$-intercept is $5$. The value $-11$ results from using slope $\\frac{4}{3}$ instead of $-\\frac{4}{3}$, taking the reciprocal without negating; this gives $y + 3 = \\frac{4}{3}(x - 6) = \\frac{4}{3}x - 8$, so $y = \\frac{4}{3}x - 11$ with $y$-intercept $-11$. The value $-5$ results from a sign error, negating the correct $y$-intercept. The value $8$ results from expanding $-\\frac{4}{3}(x - 6)$ to get $-\\frac{4}{3}x + 8$ and reporting $8$ as the $y$-intercept without subtracting $3$ from both sides."}
{"id": "SATM28Y4RHS", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(1, 3)$ and $(4, 9)$. Which of the following is the equation of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 2x + 1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 2x - 1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = 3x$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $y = 2x + 1$. The slope is $\\frac{9 - 3}{4 - 1} = \\frac{6}{3} = 2$. Using point-slope form with $(1, 3)$: $y - 3 = 2(x - 1)$, which simplifies to $y = 2x + 1$. Substituting both points confirms: $2(1) + 1 = 3$ and $2(4) + 1 = 9$. The equation $y = 2x - 1$ has the correct slope but a sign error in computing the $y$-intercept. The equation $y = 3x$ results from an arithmetic error in the slope calculation. The equation $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2}$ uses the reciprocal of the slope, confusing $\\frac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta y}$ with $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$."}
{"id": "SATM2909C68", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $y$-intercept of the line $5x - 2y = 19$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\frac{19}{2}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{19}{2}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{19}{5}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-\\frac{5}{2}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $-\\frac{19}{2}$. To find the $y$-intercept, set $x = 0$: $5(0) - 2y = 19$, so $-2y = 19$, giving $y = -\\frac{19}{2}$. The distractor $\\frac{19}{2}$ results from dropping the negative sign when dividing by $-2$. The distractor $\\frac{19}{5}$ comes from setting $y = 0$ and solving for $x$, which gives the $x$-intercept instead. The distractor $-\\frac{5}{2}$ confuses the $y$-intercept with the slope of the line."}
