{"id": "SATM2AIWJ5N", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $x$-intercept of the line $2x + 4y = 12$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(3, 0)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(6, 0)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(0, 3)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(0, 6)$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The $x$-intercept occurs where $y = 0$. Substituting into $2x + 4(0) = 12$ gives $2x = 12$, so $x = 6$, and the $x$-intercept is $(6, 0)$. The point $(3, 0)$ results from dividing $12$ by $4$ instead of by $2$. The point $(0, 3)$ is the $y$-intercept, not the $x$-intercept. The point $(0, 6)$ incorrectly places the $x$-value of $6$ in the $y$-coordinate."}
{"id": "SATM2AKVMYX", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Line $p$ passes through the points $(2, 13)$ and $(7, 43)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $p$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "1", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{43 - 13}{7 - 2} = \\frac{30}{5} = 6$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 13)$: $y - 13 = 6(x - 2)$, which gives $y = 6x - 12 + 13 = 6x + 1$. Setting $x = 0$ yields $y = 1$, so the $y$-intercept is 1."}
{"id": "SATM2ALFTAU", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The equation $9x + 4y = 36$ represents a line. What is the $x$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(4, 0)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(0, 9)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(9, 0)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(0, 4)$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The $x$-intercept occurs where $y = 0$. Substituting: $9x + 4(0) = 36$, so $9x = 36$ and $x = 4$. The $x$-intercept is $(4, 0)$. The point $(0, 9)$ confuses the $x$-intercept with the $y$-intercept and computes $36/4 = 9$ on the wrong axis. The point $(9, 0)$ uses the coefficient of $x$ as the $x$-coordinate rather than solving the equation. The point $(0, 4)$ correctly computes $36/9 = 4$ but places it on the $y$-axis instead of the $x$-axis."}
{"id": "SATM2AR2PYR", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(1, 11)$ and $(4, 2)$. Which of the following is the equation of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = -3x + 14$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 3x + 8$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -3x + 8$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = -3x - 14$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{2 - 11}{4 - 1} = \\frac{-9}{3} = -3$. Using point-slope form with $(1, 11)$: $y - 11 = -3(x - 1)$, so $y = -3x + 3 + 11 = -3x + 14$. The equation $y = 3x + 8$ uses a positive slope of $3$ instead of $-3$, a sign error. The equation $y = -3x + 8$ has the correct slope but an arithmetic slip when computing the $y$-intercept. The equation $y = -3x - 14$ applies a sign error to the constant term."}
{"id": "SATM2ASIA6B", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(1, 9)$ and $(4, 21)$. What is the slope of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$12$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-4$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{21 - 9}{4 - 1} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$. The value $3$ results from inverting the fraction as $\\frac{3}{12}$ and then simplifying incorrectly. The value $12$ comes from computing only the numerator $21 - 9$ without dividing by the change in $x$. The value $-4$ is a sign error from reversing the order of subtraction in one coordinate but not the other."}
