{"id": "SATM2BDVMM9", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ passes through the point $(4, -1)$ and is perpendicular to the line $5x + 2y = 9$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $p$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-13/5", "explanation": "Rewriting $5x + 2y = 9$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = -\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{9}{2}$, so its slope is $-\\frac{5}{2}$. A line perpendicular to this has slope $\\frac{2}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(4, -1)$: $y - (-1) = \\frac{2}{5}(x - 4)$, which gives $y + 1 = \\frac{2}{5}x - \\frac{8}{5}$. Solving for $y$: $y = \\frac{2}{5}x - \\frac{8}{5} - \\frac{5}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}x - \\frac{13}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{13}{5}$."}
