{"id": "SATM2BI4AV1", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(-2, 11)$ and $(4, -7)$. What is the slope of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{1}{3}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{2}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{-7 - 11}{4 - (-2)} = \\frac{-18}{6} = -3$. The value $3$ results from dropping the negative sign when computing the numerator or denominator. The value $-\\frac{1}{3}$ comes from inverting the fraction, computing $\\frac{6}{-18}$ instead of $\\frac{-18}{6}$. The value $\\frac{2}{3}$ comes from incorrectly calculating the differences as $\\frac{4}{6}$ or a similar setup error."}
{"id": "SATM2BKYY8O", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 11)$ and $(5, 23)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "3", "explanation": "The slope of the line is $\\frac{23 - 11}{5 - 2} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$. Using point-slope form with the point $(2, 11)$: $y - 11 = 4(x - 2)$, which simplifies to $y = 4x + 3$. The $y$-intercept is $3$. A common error is computing the slope as $\\frac{5 - 2}{23 - 11} = \\frac{1}{4}$, which inverts the rise and run. Another error is stopping at the slope without finding the intercept."}
{"id": "SATM2BLH14G", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x - 2y = 18$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point where line $p$ intersects the $y$-axis. What is the equation of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$2x + 5y = -45$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$2x - 5y = 45$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5x + 2y = -18$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$2x + 5y = 45$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The $y$-intercept of line $p$ is found by setting $x = 0$: $-2y = 18$, so $y = -9$. The point is $(0, -9)$. The slope of line $p$ is $\\frac{5}{2}$, so the perpendicular slope is $-\\frac{2}{5}$. Line $q$: $y - (-9) = -\\frac{2}{5}(x - 0)$, giving $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x - 9$. Multiplying by $5$: $5y = -2x - 45$, or $2x + 5y = -45$. The distractor $2x - 5y = 45$ has slope $\\frac{2}{5}$ (positive instead of negative), omitting the sign change in the negative reciprocal. The distractor $5x + 2y = -18$ uses the original coefficients negated rather than the reciprocal, yielding slope $-\\frac{5}{2}$ instead of $-\\frac{2}{5}$. The distractor $2x + 5y = 45$ has the correct slope but the wrong constant, as if the $y$-intercept were $+9$ instead of $-9$."}
{"id": "SATM2BMBJ6D", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, -3)$ and $(8, 15)$. What is the equation of this line in slope-intercept form?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 3x - 9$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 3x + 9$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -3x + 3$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = 2x - 7$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $y = 3x - 9$. The slope is $m = \\frac{15 - (-3)}{8 - 2} = \\frac{18}{6} = 3$. Using point-slope form with $(2, -3)$: $y - (-3) = 3(x - 2)$, which gives $y + 3 = 3x - 6$, so $y = 3x - 9$. The equation $y = 3x + 9$ has the correct slope but a sign error in the intercept calculation. The equation $y = -3x + 3$ uses the negative of the correct slope. The equation $y = 2x - 7$ comes from an arithmetic slip in computing the slope as $\\frac{18}{8}$ rounded incorrectly."}
{"id": "SATM2BTMCKE", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $y$-intercept of the line $7x - 4y = 28$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$28$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Setting $x = 0$ in $7x - 4y = 28$ gives $-4y = 28$, so $y = -7$. The $y$-intercept is $-7$. The value $4$ comes from finding the $x$-intercept instead: setting $y = 0$ gives $7x = 28$, so $x = 4$. The value $7$ results from dividing $28$ by $4$ but dropping the negative sign. The value $28$ comes from reading the constant on the right side of the equation without solving for $y$."}
