{"id": "SATM2C1102R", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $3x - 9y = 27$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(6, -1)$. What is the equation of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = -3x + 17$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 3x - 19$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -3x + 19$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = \\frac{1}{3}x - 3$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "First, rewrite line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $3x - 9y = 27$ becomes $-9y = -3x + 27$, so $y = \\frac{1}{3}x - 3$. The slope of line $p$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$. A line perpendicular to line $p$ has slope $-3$ (the negative reciprocal). Using point-slope form with $(6, -1)$: $y - (-1) = -3(x - 6)$, which gives $y + 1 = -3x + 18$, so $y = -3x + 17$. The equation $y = 3x - 19$ results from using the reciprocal $3$ without negating, a sign error. The equation $y = -3x + 19$ uses the correct slope but makes an arithmetic error when simplifying $y + 1 = -3x + 18$, adding $1$ instead of subtracting it. The equation $y = \\frac{1}{3}x - 3$ is simply line $p$ itself, reflecting a failure to find the perpendicular slope."}
{"id": "SATM2C1Y2XZ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $x$-intercept of the line $3x - 7y = 42$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(6, 0)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(0, -6)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(14, 0)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(0, 14)$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "To find the $x$-intercept, set $y = 0$: $3x - 7(0) = 42$, so $3x = 42$ and $x = 14$. The $x$-intercept is $(14, 0)$. The point $(6, 0)$ results from dividing $42$ by $7$ instead of $3$, mixing up coefficients. The point $(0, -6)$ is the $y$-intercept ($-7y = 42$, $y = -6$), not the $x$-intercept \u2014 a concept confusion. The point $(0, 14)$ places the correct $x$-value at the wrong intercept type."}
{"id": "SATM2C58A21", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line is perpendicular to the line $3x - 7y = 14$ and passes through the point $(5, -2)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line? Express your answer as a fraction.", "options": [], "correct_answer": "29/3", "explanation": "First, rewrite $3x - 7y = 14$ in slope-intercept form: $y = \\frac{3}{7}x - 2$, so the slope is $\\frac{3}{7}$. A perpendicular line has slope equal to the negative reciprocal, which is $-\\frac{7}{3}$. Using the point $(5, -2)$ in point-slope form: $y - (-2) = -\\frac{7}{3}(x - 5)$, giving $y + 2 = -\\frac{7}{3}x + \\frac{35}{3}$. Subtracting $2$ (i.e., $\\frac{6}{3}$) from both sides yields $y = -\\frac{7}{3}x + \\frac{29}{3}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{29}{3}$. A common error is using $\\frac{3}{7}$ (the original slope) rather than the negative reciprocal, or making a sign error when computing the perpendicular slope."}
{"id": "SATM2CJGV14", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $7x + 3y = 41$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(7, -2)$. What is the $y$-coordinate of the point where line $q$ crosses the $y$-axis?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-5", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $3y = -7x + 41$, so $y = -\\frac{7}{3}x + \\frac{41}{3}$. The slope of line $p$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}$. A line perpendicular to $p$ has slope equal to the negative reciprocal, which is $\\frac{3}{7}$. Using point-slope form with the point $(7, -2)$: $y - (-2) = \\frac{3}{7}(x - 7)$, giving $y + 2 = \\frac{3}{7}x - 3$, so $y = \\frac{3}{7}x - 5$. At the $y$-axis, $x = 0$, so $y = -5$."}
