{"id": "SATM2D7M5TR", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x + 3y = 21$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(4, -1)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-17/5", "explanation": "The slope of line $p$ is $-\\frac{5}{3}$ (rewriting as $y = -\\frac{5}{3}x + 7$). A line perpendicular to $p$ has slope $\\frac{3}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(4, -1)$: $y - (-1) = \\frac{3}{5}(x - 4)$, which gives $y + 1 = \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{12}{5}$. So $y = \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{12}{5} - 1 = \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{17}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{17}{5}$."}
