{"id": "SATM2EZVLK3", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 5)$ and $(6, 13)$. What is the slope of the line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$2$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$8$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{13 - 5}{6 - 2} = \\frac{8}{4} = 2$. The value $\\frac{1}{2}$ results from inverting the slope formula, computing $\\frac{6 - 2}{13 - 5}$ instead. The value $4$ comes from using only the denominator of the slope fraction (the change in $x$). The value $8$ comes from computing only the numerator (the change in $y$) without dividing by the change in $x$."}
{"id": "SATM2F00ERX", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $4x + 7y = 53$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(8, -2)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-16", "explanation": "First, find the slope of line $p$ by rewriting in slope-intercept form: $7y = -4x + 53$, so $y = -\\frac{4}{7}x + \\frac{53}{7}$. The slope of line $p$ is $-\\frac{4}{7}$. A line perpendicular to $p$ has slope $\\frac{7}{4}$ (the negative reciprocal). Using point-slope form with $(8, -2)$: $y - (-2) = \\frac{7}{4}(x - 8)$, which gives $y + 2 = \\frac{7}{4}x - 14$, so $y = \\frac{7}{4}x - 16$. The $y$-intercept is $-16$."}
